WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.458
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: When it
comes migrating to Google Cloud,

00:00:02.458 --> 00:00:04.800
how do you build connectivity
between your on-premises

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.360
and Google Cloud environment?

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.572
How do you secure and
monitor your apps?

00:00:08.572 --> 00:00:09.780
Well, stay tuned to find out.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.636
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:21.400
Welcome to Cloud Migration 101,
where we share migration tips

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:21.910
and tricks.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:24.820
In the last episode, we learned
about resource hierarchies,

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:27.400
access control,
permissions using IAM,

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.860
and the four methods of
interacting with Google Cloud

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:30.760
services.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.560
Today, let's address
some of the questions

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:33.820
from the engineering teams.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.230
For this, let's get Kyle back.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:37.690
KYLE MOSCHETTO: Hi, I'm Kyle.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:40.702
Today, we are going to focus
on the three main questions

00:00:40.702 --> 00:00:42.160
that the engineering
team will have

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.970
when planning a migration or
landing zone on Google Cloud.

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:50.190
One, how to connect the existing
infrastructure to Google Cloud.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.510
Two, how to protect
the applications

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:54.010
to maximize security.

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:57.050
And three, how to know
when something goes wrong,

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:58.420
which is monitoring.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:00.340
Let's start with networking.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:02.080
Your existing
network architecture

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.970
and application
flow will determine

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.820
how you establish your
cloud network setup

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.640
and how your applications
communicate with your existing

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.020
services.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.120
In Google Cloud, the
physical network layer

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.270
is virtualized using
software-defined networking.

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:18.400
You will deploy and
configure a network

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.760
in a virtual private
cloud, or VPC.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:25.240
You can run VPCs across multiple
regions and multiple zones

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.240
within regions to support a
wide variety of architectures,

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.370
including backup and
disaster recovery needs.

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:35.140
You can also use a special
type of VPC called a shared VPC

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.080
to connect resources from
multiple Cloud projects

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.180
into a central VPC network.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.910
Or you can use VPC
peering to connect

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:45.790
multiple VPCs across
many different projects

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:47.560
or organizations.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.380
The next question is,
once you have a VPC,

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:54.110
how do we connect it to your on
premises existing environment?

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:56.090
There are three ways
that you can do this.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:58.690
First, you can connect
over the public internet

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:00.460
using Cloud VPN.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.300
Second, you can connect over a
private, SLA-backed, dedicated

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:07.130
high-speed connection
using Cloud Interconnect.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:10.000
Or third, you can establish
a direct peering connection

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.830
with Google's Edge network.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:14.050
Typically, you would
set up Cloud VPNs

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:17.350
for testing and development
or temporary connections,

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:20.380
and establish interconnects
for production environments

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.890
or to support the widespread
migration of your applications

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:25.600
to Google Cloud.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.880
The next question that I
commonly hear from customers

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.490
is, I have a firewall
that currently secures

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:32.420
my entire corporate network.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.990
How can I migrate this
firewall to Google Cloud?

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:36.490
The way that you
manage and maintain

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:39.220
the security of your
systems in Google Cloud

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:40.990
can be different
than when managing

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:42.760
an on-premises infrastructure.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.580
We highly recommend you check
out the firewall and security

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.770
features available in your VPC.

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:48.580
Link below.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:51.580
Google Cloud is designed to
support a zero trust security

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:54.640
model, which can now be
enabled at your organization

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:58.300
with our context-aware
security and access solutions.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:01.390
As far as data is concerned,
Google Cloud encrypts your data

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:03.590
in transit and at rest.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:07.090
You also have other options like
Cloud Key Management Service

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:09.280
and cloud hardware
security modules,

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:12.920
depending on your security
and regulatory requirements.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:14.560
And there are a
variety of options

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.800
to secure your infrastructure,
networks, endpoints,

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:19.180
and applications.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:20.950
Check out the links
below to learn more

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:24.420
about the specific benefits
of Google Cloud Security.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:26.280
The next question
I usually get is,

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:30.360
how do my existing monitoring
tools work within Google Cloud?

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.300
If you are using any VMware
or software-based system,

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.490
you can always just lift
and shift it directly

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:38.670
to Google's Compute Engine
or Google Kubernetes Engine

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:42.720
and continue to use it
exactly as you do on-premises.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.940
For workloads running
on Google Cloud,

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:47.250
you can use a fully
managed monitoring

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:49.890
and logging solution called
Cloud Monitoring and Cloud

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:51.240
Logging.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.360
You can even export monitoring
and logging events easily

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:57.570
into BigQuery and run all
kinds of reporting and analysis

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:58.080
on them.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.140
As an example, you can
move a workload to GCE,

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.890
run a new GKE cluster, and set
up some serverless functions

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.990
and have all of those
logs automatically sent

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:08.190
to Cloud Logging.

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:09.973
Priyanka, where you at?

00:04:09.973 --> 00:04:10.890
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK.

